T=[('Hemispheric state.','Unihemispheric slow-wave sleep combines stronger slow waves on one side with waking-like activity on the other. Bilateral EEG distinguishes this state from eye closure alone.'),('Comparative anatomy.','Cetaceans retain familiar cholinergic, monoaminergic, and orexinergic systems. Anatomical differences supply mechanistic hypotheses, while chemical release and EEG measure physiological state.'),('Chemical specificity.','Fur-seal cortical acetylcholine is lateralized with EEG activation; measured serotonin, noradrenaline, and histamine do not acquire the same consistent hemispheric separation.'),('Vigilance and sleep pressure.','Mallards adjust sleep and eye direction to group exposure. Prior visual use changes local pigeon sleep, while higher jackdaw sleep pressure favors more symmetric sleep.'),('Aquatic and aerial alternatives.','Fur seals favor asymmetric surface sleep, elephant seals sleep bilaterally during deep dives, and frigatebirds express both forms during flight while greatly reducing daily sleep.'),('Neural boundaries.','Local EEG state, ongoing movement, sensory responses, awakening, and conscious reporting are different measurements. Cross-species comparisons preserve these distinctions.')]
